  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nThe high pressure system that has been in place the last few \ndays will gradually fill and move slowly to the Northwest as the\ncoastal low deepens today off the Mid Atlantic coast. High \nclouds fill in from east to west, filtering the sun by the \nafternoon. The high clouds as well as increasing northerly winds\nwill help keep tonight's lows 5 to 10 degrees warmer than the \nlast couple of nights. \n\nThe coastal low still has a significant amount of uncertainty \nwith the track despite impacts 24 to 36 hours out. The main \nreason is this low looks to be a bit of a hybrid between a \ntropical low and a mid latitude cyclone. Deep convection around \nthe core will help with pressure falls while the cool dry air \nmass and tropical airmass of the coast will provide \nbaroclinicity for pressure falls and cyclogenesis. The \ncombination of a baroclinic process and latent heat release \nprocess is something that models do not handle well until the \nlow has consolidated. Even the Convective allowing models \nstruggle mainly because the low is on the edge of their domains \nand they tend to over do deep moist convection over warm waters.\nTonight's model runs in both deterministic and ensemble \nguidance that has come in has brought the low farther south and \nwest, with occlusion likely happening off the Mid Atlantic \ncoast. QPF and chances of precipitation have been raised \nslightly region wide. The Catskills may get a bit of a rainfall \nbullseye with how far west the low looks to potentially end up \nwith moist NE flow. Even under some of the wetter solutions with\n2 to 3 inches of rain over 24 to 36 hours, rainfall intensity \nis below flash flood concerns and amounts are not of concern for\nriver flooding either. \n\nWithout much synoptic support due to a weak jet stream and the \nlow moving into cooler waters reducing the deep convection near \nthe center. This likely causes the low to occlude quickly late \nSunday into Monday morning with only a few light lingering rain \nshowers by Sunrise Monday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \n\nOnce the low fills and becomes an open wave Monday, Upper level\nridging starts to build in with initially Zonal flow becoming \nSouthwest flow by Mid week. A surface high in place coupled with\nsome subsidence aloft under the ridge means chances of \nprecipitation are near 0 late Monday through Wednesday. Towards \nlate week, a few shortwaves look to try and sneak into the \nNortheast in the SW flow to increase chances of rain slightly. \nTemperatures will also be slowly warming, getting back above \naverage by mid week.",
